BLOCK|h1|1|Cluster-lang: Chapter 6 — Async Programming & Metaprogramming
BLOCK|p||This chapter covers asynchronous programming (`async`/`await`), decorators, and reflection in Cluster-lang.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|1. Asynchronous Programming (`async` / `await`)
BLOCK|p||For high-throughput, non-blocking I/O tasks, Cluster-lang provides async coroutines.
BLOCK|h3|3|Defining Async Functions
BLOCK|p||Define an async function by prefixing it with the `async` keyword:
BLOCK|code|python|async fn fetch_data(url: string) -> string:
    // Simulated network I/O
    delay(100)
    return "Data from " + url
BLOCK|h3|3|Awaiting Async Functions
BLOCK|p||To suspend execution until an async task finishes, use the `await` keyword:
BLOCK|code|python|fn main():
    task := fetch_data("https://api.example.com")
    result := await task
    put result
BLOCK|p||Under the hood, async operations are transpiled to cooperative C++ futures and coroutines using modern C++20 `std::coroutine`.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|2. Metaprogramming & Decorators
BLOCK|p||Decorators allow you to modify or monitor the behavior of functions and models at compile-time or runtime.
BLOCK|h3|3|Using Decorators
BLOCK|p||Decorators are prefixed with the `@` symbol and placed directly above function declarations:
BLOCK|code|python|@log_execution
fn process(data: int) -> int:
    return data * 2
BLOCK|h3|3|Property Descriptors
BLOCK|p||You can use decorators to attach properties, getters, or setters to model fields:
BLOCK|code|python|model Product:
    @readonly
    id: int
    
    @property
    price: float
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|3. Reflection & Introspection
BLOCK|p||Cluster-lang provides metadata introspection capabilities so program structures can query their own attributes at runtime.
BLOCK|h3|3|Inspecting Models
BLOCK|p||You can retrieve the name, fields, and types of fields on any model instance:
BLOCK|code|python|model User:
    name: string
    age: int

fn main():
    u := box User(name="Alice", age=30)
    
    // Introspection query
    put u.__name__            // Prints "User"
    put u.__fields__          // Prints ["name", "age"]
BLOCK|p||This is extremely useful for building automatic JSON serializers/deserializers, database ORMs, and loggers.
BLOCK|hr||
